#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122217e5-263b-5c2f-a1a6-72cd3fe09ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238207} {:answer/id #uuid "1d352e77-efb7-5062-9266-9d12f3ebc8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238301} {:answer/id #uuid "27dca027-745d-5b20-9ded-c32694cd03a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238400} {:answer/id #uuid "6a625d4b-15d9-5410-ba57-4b7cb980b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238978} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1d6d40-def6-5729-9987-cad555a6c07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27dca027-745d-5b20-9ded-c32694cd03a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238400} :answer-field/id #uuid "25bd6724-359f-5d1b-93b9-7b2ffa2d4f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54529993-0bd1-511f-b362-f0eafc672ef8" :question/math-academy-id "q-105671" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c38294780ac6d5079b759f39a7dbd4944b1c3c21495245dfd314ec180c0434e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/97/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddd69671-8e80-570d-8d75-96f25bae826b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130727} {:answer/id #uuid "1810f53a-f655-5e06-924f-8a0000e9c96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130728} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd952dd-30a7-574a-a909-7528696b5bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130729} {:answer/id #uuid "451df22b-6f12-596b-9272-9b475aa0031a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130730} {:answer/id #uuid "75558a5f-b4bc-523b-b1c5-e6560aa8f362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130731}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e34b372f-2e0b-5cd6-8b2c-dd09b2ed954b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130726}] :question/id #uuid "494f0061-8555-5747-be24-aea11c4501ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-126428" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = x\\sin yi + \\cos (xy)j$. Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(π, 0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13264dd3-32ca-5425-ae4a-10ae592d25f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260044} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4275e-e84f-5e99-9926-6ed1c4bda7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec4d041-01f8-5cba-bbc5-711d818eb654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260692} {:answer/id #uuid "7344edf5-4fa2-53cc-aefd-2082b97c5134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260883} {:answer/id #uuid "deb142df-e54d-5f60-a501-f62bc2ccccc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4275e-e84f-5e99-9926-6ed1c4bda7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} :answer-field/id #uuid "6208b069-a1cc-5b39-96a3-5295dfbd12c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84980841-460b-507f-8790-439efc8ff63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126525" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,i+(x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(0,\\pi /2)} & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
